  "stopFeatures": [],
  "notes": "Zones from four shapefiles sent by Greater Portland Metro (Director of Transit Development) to Atlas Outreach on 2026-11-16: 'Falmouth Metro Connect.zip', 'Scarborough Metro Connect.zip', 'Scarborough Metro Connect 2.zip' and 'Scarborough Metro Connect 3.zip', described as all of Metro's microtransit zone geometry as of that day. Geometry used exactly as given (WGS84 in the files). Falmouth is one polygon (about 42 km2). Scarborough is drawn as one MultiPolygon: the main zone (about 36 km2) plus the two small add-on areas from files 2 and 3 (about 0.05 km2 and 0.003 km2), both detached and just south of the main zone (on the OpenStreetMap basemap, the larger one outlines the Piper Shores main building off Piper Road and the smaller one sits at Black Point Road and Sprague Way); the agency names them as part of Scarborough Metro Connect. Source caveats from the agency: the boundaries are subject to change without warning, usually expanding slightly to accommodate specific requests, and a larger expansion may come in 2027. Metro is working on publishing the zones as a public GTFS-Flex feed; Metro's GTFS has no Flex yet, so re-check against it once it appears. General public, not paratransit: the Falmouth page calls it 'an on-demand public transit service' for residents and visitors, the Scarborough page says rides cost a standard bus fare, and the main page lists no eligibility rule (ADA paratransit is a separate service). Hours from the main Metro Connect page (they match the Falmouth and Scarborough pages); no holiday hours are stated there. Pickup: the main page says riders board at virtual stops, usually under a five-minute walk; the Scarborough page says 'doorstep-to-destination', so the main page's wording is used. Riders connect to Routes 7 and 9B in Falmouth and Route 25 (and BSOOB Transit Route 60) in Scarborough."
